    "image_caption": null,
    "content_html": "<div class=\"articlebodycontent col-xl-9 col-lg-12 col-md-12 col-sm-12 col-12\" id=\"content-body-71736073\"><div class=\"schemaDiv\" id=\"schemaDiv\"><p>In his seminal book <i>Universities in the Marketplace: The Commercialization of Higher Education</i>, Derek Bok, the former President of Harvard University, highlighted the danger of Higher Education Institutions (HEIs) getting transformed into entrepreneurial entities — aggressively pursuing market revenues that dilute and jeopardise educational excellence. Indian HEIs too, such as the IITs and IIMs, spurred by the exuberance of their freshly started peers, have championed and commenced new courses, begun expanding faculty and opened new centres. While this helps them to become more profitable in the short run, it also exposes them to operational risks when governance systems are weak. In an age of AI, even the slightest infraction can cause instant and even lasting reputational damage. In order to sustain HEIs’ credentials, the government must ensure their governance systems stay decisively robust.</p><p>In recent months, there has been much discourse about tightening the entrance examination process in India. However, one mustn’t forget that past examination and recruitment scams in India such as Vyapam, NEET-UG etc. occurred only because technological fail-safes were defeated by human subterfuge; key officials in control of the operational systems gamed them to their advantage. HEIs are complex bodies that deal with much more than examination processes. Are we sure that those charged with their governance are impeccable in their intent?</p></div><div class=\"articleblock-container\" id=\"arthardpv\"></div><div class=\"update-publish-time article-published-time-end\"><p class=\"publish-time-new\"> Published <span>- November 24, 2026 01:15 am IST</span></p></div><div class=\"comments-shares share-page flex print-hide\" data-artid=\"71736073\" data-title=\"Higher education needs to centre mer